     "role": "assistant",
     "content": "Why Most Product Roadmaps Fail\n\nThe roadmap was supposed to bring clarity. Instead, three months later no one trusts it, no one follows it, and everyone quietly builds something else. This is the norm, not the exception. Most product roadmaps fail — and it’s rarely because teams aren’t working hard.\n\nFirst, we confuse a roadmap with a release plan. A classic roadmap is a timeline packed with features and dates: “Mobile checkout in Q2, AI search in Q3.” It looks decisive, but it promises certainty we don’t have. When discovery takes longer, dependencies slip, or customers want something else, the entire plan collapses. Leaders see missed dates as failure, engineers feel set up, and trust evaporates.\n\nSecond, most roadmaps measure output, not outcome. They list what we will ship, not why it matters or what will change for the user and business. Without a clear problem to solve and metric to move, there’s no way to say no, to prioritize, or to know if we succeeded. Success becomes “we shipped on time,” even if no one uses it. That turns product teams into feature factories.\n\nThird, there is no strategy underneath. A good roadmap is a story about how we win: who we serve, what pain we uniquely solve, what we will not do. Too many roadmaps skip that and become a negotiated list of stakeholder requests. Sales wants this enterprise feature, Support wants that fix, the CEO saw a competitor launch something shiny. When a roadmap tries to please everyone, it serves no one. It’s political compromise disguised as a plan.\n\nFourth, roadmaps are treated as static contracts in a dynamic world. Markets shift, data arrives, assumptions prove wrong. But updating the roadmap feels like admitting failure, so teams either cling to an outdated plan or abandon it entirely. Both kill credibility.\n\nFinally, most roadmaps are never truly communicated. They live in a slide deck, full of jargon, with no shared understanding of trade-offs. Engineering doesn’t believe the dates, design wasn’t involved early, customers were never consulted.\n\nThe roadmaps that work do the opposite: they anchor on outcomes, embrace uncertainty, make hard choices explicit, and are revisited continuously. They are not promises of features. They are bets on how to create value — and like all bets, they are meant to be learned from, not just delivered.",
